  "title": "Closing the Diagnostic Loop: Spectral-Guided Adaptive Data Mixing for Model Collapse Prevention",
  "core_problem": "Model collapse during recursive synthetic training is increasingly well-diagnosed — SIGMA tracks log-determinant spectral contraction of embedding Gram matrices, calibration-based monitoring detects degradation before accuracy loss, topology-based methods detect representational anisotropy — and theoretically optimal static mixing ratios have been derived (converging to the reciprocal of the golden ratio in simplified settings). Meanwhile, adaptive data selection methods like DCES address collapse through intra-cluster rebalancing within the synthetic pool. Yet no existing system closes the loop from spectral diagnosis to adaptive global real/synthetic mixing. Static mixing ratios, whether theoretically optimal or empirically tuned, assume the collapse pressure is constant across training — but empirical evidence consistently shows otherwise: SIGMA's S2 experiments demonstrate that spectral contraction accelerates non-linearly, with the synthetic pool 'losing whole modes' around generation 20 and Track II drift intensifying sharply thereafter; Shumailov et al. (Nature 2024) show that tail collapse compounds across generations with longer error tails appearing in later generations; and arXiv 2510.05133 identifies inflection points at 20-25% synthetic data proportion where per-unit degradation rates double from 0.1-0.2 to 0.4-0.8 percentage points. This generation-dependent, non-linear collapse pressure is precisely what makes static mixing suboptimal — and what motivates a closed-loop controller that concentrates real data where collapse pressure is highest.",
  "opportunity": "SIGMA (2026) provides scalable spectral monitoring via embedding Gram matrix log-determinants during recursive training, but is purely diagnostic — it tracks collapse without prescribing interventions. Crucially, SIGMA's S2 experiments on OPT-125M across 50 recursive generations reveal that spectral contraction is non-linear and accelerating: Track II shows a final drift of approximately -1536.562 with slope -42.621, and around generation 20 the synthetic pool begins losing whole modes, with the metric dropping much faster — demonstrating clear generational acceleration rather than linear degradation. This non-linearity is the linchpin of our contribution: if collapse pressure were constant across generations, static mixing would be near-optimal. But the empirical evidence consistently shows acceleration. Shumailov et al. (Nature 2024) demonstrate that tail collapse compounds across generations — early model collapse is subtle (losing tail information), while late collapse is catastrophic (producing samples never seen in the original distribution), with longer error tails appearing in later generations. The calibration-based monitoring literature corroborates this: arXiv 2510.05133 finds that degradation below 20-25% synthetic data averages 0.1-0.2 percentage points per unit synthetic proportion, but accelerates to 0.4-0.8 pp/pp beyond 30%, with ECE increasing from 0.08 to 0.14 at iteration 1 (while accuracy remains flat) and reaching 0.21-0.28 in subsequent iterations as accuracy degrades sharply. The Knowledge Collapse study (arXiv 2509.04796) further confirms three distinct non-linear stages: knowledge preservation, knowledge collapse ('confidently wrong'), and instruction-following collapse — with prompt-dependent transition points rather than uniform degradation. The theoretical static-mixing literature has matured rapidly: Golden Ratio Weighting (He et al. 2025) proves the optimal real-data weight converges to 1/phi approx 0.618 across Gaussian, GLM, and nonparametric settings; Garg et al. (2025) extend this to ridge regression under overparametrization, showing optimal mixing >= 0.5; and recent theoretical work on iterative training with contaminated data (arXiv 2602.10531) shows that even decaying real-data mixing weights can avoid collapse under appropriate sample size conditions (sum alpha_t = infinity, sum 1/n_t < infinity), providing theoretical grounding for dynamic mixing strategies. However, these theoretical optima apply to idealized settings with constant collapse pressure — they cannot account for the empirically observed generation-dependent acceleration. The closest adaptive approach is DCES (Dynamic Center-Edge Sampling, MDPI Electronics 2026), which uses intra-cluster dispersion to adaptively rebalance center-edge sampling ratios within the synthetic pool — but DCES operates at the sample selection level within the synthetic data, not at the global real/synthetic mixing ratio level, and does not connect to spectral Gram matrix health or theoretical mixing optima. Alternative prevention strategies — data accumulation (Gerstgrasser et al. 2024), verification (He et al. 2025), pluralistic curation (Falahati et al. 2026) — address collapse through data quality or quantity but not through adaptive real-time mixing control. The gap: connecting spectral degradation monitoring to adaptive global data mixing decisions in a closed-loop system that dynamically allocates a fixed real data budget across recursive training generations, exploiting the empirically established non-linearity of collapse pressure.",
  "landscape": "SIGMA (arXiv 2601.03385, Mar 2026) benchmarks model collapse through embedding Gram matrix spectral analysis, deriving deterministic bounds (Track I: log det(I_m + G_a/(beta_hat+delta)), computed efficiently via Cholesky decomposition on shifted PSD matrices, where beta_hat_k = (n_k - n_a)*rho is the tail-energy budget estimator) and stochastic bounds (Track II). Validated on OPT-125M across 50 recursive generations using a frozen sentence encoder with embedding dimension m=384. The framework is 'black-box friendly' requiring only output embeddings — the frozen sentence encoder encodes generated text rather than internal activations, making it architecturally portable in principle. However, the Track I bound's calibration (beta_hat estimator relying on rho = maximum embedding norm squared, and delta regularization) was tuned for OPT-125M outputs, and may require re-calibration for different architectures (e.g., Pythia) whose output distributions could differ in scale and geometry. No public code repository is available as of May 2026. DCES (Dynamic Center-Edge Sampling, MDPI Electronics 15(4):869, Feb 2026) is the closest adaptive approach to collapse prevention: it monitors intra-cluster dispersion of semantic clusters and dynamically adjusts center-edge sampling ratios during iterative self-training, tested on GPT-2, Qwen, and OPT-6.7B across 10 rounds. Key distinctions from our proposal: (a) DCES adapts sample selection within the synthetic pool (which center vs. edge samples to include), whereas we adapt the global real/synthetic mixing ratio; (b) DCES uses semantic cluster dispersion while we use mathematically grounded spectral Gram matrix bounds; (c) our method connects to theoretical mixing optima (golden ratio) that DCES does not; (d) DCES's token-level editing and anchor-model filtering are complementary mechanisms that could be combined with spectral-guided mixing in future work. Characterizing Model Behavior Under Synthetic Data Training (arXiv 2510.05133, Oct 2025) demonstrates on Pythia 410M-12B that calibration degradation (ECE) precedes accuracy loss by 1-2 iterations with 91% precision and 87% recall using a 50% ECE increase threshold — and critically shows that degradation accelerates non-linearly beyond 20-25% synthetic data proportion (from 0.1-0.2 to 0.4-0.8 pp/pp), providing both a competing monitoring signal and direct empirical evidence for generation-dependent collapse pressure. Knowledge Collapse in LLMs (arXiv 2509.04796, Sep 2025) demonstrates three distinct non-linear collapse stages on GEMMA 3 1B under recursive synthetic training at alpha in {0.25, 0.50, 1.0}: knowledge preservation -> knowledge collapse ('confidently wrong') -> instruction-following collapse, with prompt-dependent transition points, further confirming that collapse pressure is generation-dependent and non-uniform. Strong Model Collapse (ICLR 2025) proves that even tiny synthetic fractions cause collapse under scaling. Entropy Collapse (arXiv 2512.12381, Dec 2025) proves entropy collapse is a first-order discontinuous phase transition without critical slowing down — ruling out ecological EWS but not spectral log-determinant contraction as a gradual, actionable feedback signal. Golden Ratio Weighting (arXiv 2502.18049, He et al., Feb 2025) proves the optimal real-data weight converges to 1/phi for Gaussian, GLM, and nonparametric settings — establishing theoretical static optima but assuming constant collapse pressure. Preventing Model Collapse Under Overparametrization (arXiv 2509.22341, Garg et al., Sep 2025) extends the golden ratio result to min-l2-norm interpolation and ridge regression, showing the optimal real-data proportion is >= 0.5. From Collapse to Improvement (arXiv 2602.10531, Feb 2026) analyzes iterative training on contaminated sources and proves that decaying real-data mixing weights (alpha_t -> 0) still avoid collapse when sum(alpha_t) = infinity and sample sizes grow appropriately, with the central recurrence R_t = (n_0/n_t)R_0 + ((n_{t-1})/n_t)(1-alpha_t)^2 R_{t-1} — providing direct theoretical support for dynamic mixing strategies. A Theoretical Perspective on Preventing Model Collapse in Self-consuming Training Loops (arXiv 2502.18865, ICLR 2025) introduces recursive stability theory and shows that a constant-sized proportion of real data ensures convergence, with both model architecture and data composition critically influencing STL success. Gerstgrasser et al. (2024) show data accumulation avoids collapse but treats the real/synthetic ratio as static. Curated Synthetic Data Doesn't Have to Collapse (arXiv 2605.07724, Falahati et al., May 2026) demonstrates pluralistic reward curation prevents collapse — a complementary mechanism to mixing-ratio control. Monitoring Neural Training with Topology (arXiv 2604.26984, Apr 2026) detects representational anisotropy via persistent homology but targets single-run fine-tuning. Spectral Lens (arXiv 2605.05683, May 2026) uses activation/gradient spectra for optimization diagnostics, not collapse prevention. RankMe (Garrido et al., ICML 2023) provides a well-established, hyperparameter-free, architecture-agnostic effective rank metric via spectral entropy of singular values — we use this as our primary fallback and complementary signal to SIGMA's Track I, with existing implementations readily available. The adaptive data mixing literature (Data Mixing Laws, DoReMi, arXiv 2603.14712) optimizes domain proportions using loss-based feedback but has not addressed synthetic-data collapse or spectral health signals. The specific gap: no existing work uses spectral health signals as a closed-loop feedback mechanism for adaptive global real/synthetic data mixing during recursive synthetic training.",
  "approach": "We present the first empirical study connecting spectral collapse monitoring to adaptive global real/synthetic data mixing in a closed-loop system. Targeting an EMNLP 2026 short paper, we adopt an aggressive but feasible scope. **SIGMA Reimplementation Plan**: Since no public code repository exists for SIGMA (arXiv 2601.03385) as of May 2026, we will reimplement the Track I log-determinant bound from the paper's formulas (log det(I_m + G_a/(beta_hat+delta)) via Cholesky decomposition on shifted PSD matrices, with beta_hat_k = (n_k - n_a)*rho). The math is standard linear algebra; we estimate 1-1.5 days of engineering. **SIGMA->Pythia Transfer Risk**: SIGMA was validated exclusively on OPT-125M with a frozen sentence encoder (m=384). While the sentence encoder approach is black-box (encoding generated text, not internal activations), the beta_hat estimator's calibration (rho = maximum embedding norm squared) may differ for Pythia-generated text. We mitigate this with: (a) re-calibrating delta on the first generation's outputs, and (b) using effective rank (RankMe, Garrido et al. ICML 2023) as a primary fallback — RankMe is architecture-agnostic, hyperparameter-free, and has existing implementations, providing a robust alternative if Track I calibration proves problematic across architectures. Phase 1 — Spectral and calibration contraction characterization (days 1-5): Run recursive training on Pythia 410M at 2 mixing ratios (0% synthetic = pure collapse, 50% synthetic) x 5 generations x 3 seeds = 30 generation-steps. At each generation checkpoint, compute: (a) SIGMA's Track I log-determinant bound (or effective rank fallback), (b) effective rank of the embedding Gram matrix as a complementary signal, (c) Expected Calibration Error (ECE) following arXiv 2510.05133 as a calibration-based monitoring baseline — this requires near-zero additional compute (just binning model predictions on the held-out set) and directly addresses whether spectral signals provide earlier or more fine-grained warning than simpler calibration tracking, (d) held-out perplexity and distinct-4 as downstream baselines, (e) HellaSwag accuracy (0-shot, via lm-evaluation-harness) as a downstream task quality metric — Pythia 410M achieves ~40.9% baseline (well above chance ~25%), providing meaningful dynamic range to detect collapse-induced degradation. We explicitly omit MMLU: Pythia 410M scores ~27% (near-random for 4-choice), providing insufficient dynamic range for meaningful collapse detection. Additionally, run 1 mixing ratio (0% synthetic) x 5 generations x 1 seed on Pythia 1B to validate cross-scale transfer of spectral contraction patterns (~5 generation-steps). Total Phase 1: ~35 generation-steps, ~35 H100-hours (including SIGMA reimplementation time). Phase 2 — Threshold-triggered adaptive mixing (days 5-9): Implement a threshold-proportional controller: when the generation-over-generation decline in Track I log-determinant (or effective rank) exceeds a calibrated threshold (set from Phase 1 at the inflection point where downstream perplexity and HellaSwag begin degrading), increase the real data fraction proportionally. We deliberately choose this simple controller over more complex alternatives (PID, Bayesian optimization, learned policies) because: (i) the short-paper scope requires a minimal-complexity proof of concept, (ii) the simple controller is fully interpretable and reproducible, (iii) establishing that ANY spectral-guided controller outperforms uniform static mixing is the core contribution — optimization of the controller itself is future work. Evaluate 4 conditions on Pythia 410M x 5 generations x 3 seeds = 60 generation-steps: (a) spectral-guided adaptive mixing (Track I log-determinant signal), (b) calibration-guided adaptive mixing (ECE signal, using the same threshold-proportional controller but with ECE as the feedback signal — this isolates the value of spectral vs. calibration monitoring), (c) fixed 50/50 mixing (strong static baseline, near the 1/phi theoretical optimum), (d) no intervention (pure collapse trajectory). At each generation, evaluate held-out perplexity, distinct-4, and HellaSwag accuracy. Additionally, run the spectral-guided adaptive controller on Pythia 1B x 5 generations x 1 seed = 5 generation-steps to validate that threshold calibration transfers across scales (not just spectral contraction patterns). Total Phase 2: ~65 generation-steps, ~65 H100-hours. **Statistical Power**: We increase from 2 to 3 seeds per condition to improve statistical reliability. While the model collapse literature ranges from 3 seeds (NeurIPS short papers) to 5 seeds (Shumailov et al., Nature 2024), 3 seeds represents a pragmatic minimum for a short paper within our compute budget. We adopt paired seeds across conditions (identical seeds for spectral-guided, calibration-guided, fixed 50/50, and no-intervention), following the Paired Seed Evaluation methodology (arXiv 2512.24145), which provably reduces variance and yields tighter confidence intervals when seed-level correlations are positive — as expected when the same initialization encounters different mixing strategies. We report mean +/- standard deviation across seeds and apply paired t-tests for significance. We explicitly acknowledge that 3 seeds limits our power to detect small effect sizes (<2 perplexity points), and frame this as a limitation to be addressed at larger scale. **Calibration-Parity Contingency**: If the calibration-guided controller performs comparably to the spectral-guided controller in Phase 2, the paper's contribution remains threefold: (1) the first closed-loop system connecting ANY monitoring signal to adaptive global mixing during recursive training — demonstrating that monitoring-guided dynamic allocation outperforms static mixing regardless of signal choice; (2) a practical framework for budget-efficient collapse prevention that is signal-agnostic; (3) an empirical characterization of spectral vs. calibration signal properties (granularity, lead time, smoothness for proportional control). Even under parity, the spectral signal retains theoretical advantages: it is mathematically grounded in Gram matrix theory (connected to SIGMA's spectral bounds and representation capacity), provides continuous gradients suitable for proportional control (vs. ECE's discrete threshold-based warnings), and connects to the theoretical static-mixing literature (golden ratio). Calibration (ECE) is an empirical proxy that correlates with collapse but lacks this theoretical foundation. We frame this explicitly in the paper: the controller framework is the primary contribution, with signal comparison as a secondary empirical question. Phase 3 — Analysis and writing (days 9-14): Evaluate on held-out perplexity, distinct-4, n-gram entropy, tail coverage, and HellaSwag accuracy. Key analyses: (1) Does spectral-guided dynamic mixing achieve better collapse prevention than fixed 50/50 when given the same total real data budget? The non-linearity evidence strongly supports this: SIGMA's S2 shows collapse acceleration around generation 20 (Track II drift intensifying sharply), and arXiv 2510.05133 shows degradation rates doubling beyond 20-25% synthetic proportion. If early stable generations use ~30% real data and later collapse-prone generations use ~70%, the dynamic allocation targets intervention where collapse pressure is highest, even if the average real-data usage is comparable to or below the 50% uniform baseline. (2) Does spectral monitoring (Track I log-determinant / effective rank) provide earlier or more fine-grained warning than calibration tracking (ECE)? SIGMA reports gradual, monotonic spectral contraction, while arXiv 2510.05133 shows calibration degrades in discrete steps — we hypothesize spectral signals provide smoother, more actionable gradients for proportional control. (3) Does the spectral-guided controller's threshold calibration transfer from Pythia 410M to 1B? (4) How does spectral-guided mixing compare to calibration-guided mixing when both use the same proportional controller — and what is the contribution if they perform comparably? (5) What is the minimum effect size detectable with 3 paired seeds, and does the observed advantage exceed this threshold? Position the simple controller as proof-of-concept and explicitly frame PID/Bayesian/learned-policy controllers, integration with DCES-style within-pool sample selection, larger-scale validation, and additional seeds as future work.",
  "resource_estimate": {
