  },
  "target_venues": [
    "EMNLP 2026 ARR short paper (ARR submission: May 25, 2026; commitment: August 2, 2026) — 4-page preliminary empirical study, Findings track acceptable",
    "AAAI 2027 full paper (expected ~Aug 2026) — extended version with PID/Bayesian controllers, integration with DCES-style sample selection, more model scales, additional seeds (5+), MMLU on larger models, and theoretical analysis of spectral contraction dynamics"
  ],
  "level": "solid",
  "key_references": [
    "SIGMA: Scalable Spectral Insights for LLM Model Collapse (arXiv 2601.03385, 2026) — spectral monitoring foundation; we adopt Track I log-determinant bounds directly and extend from diagnosis to closed-loop intervention. S2 experiments on OPT-125M show non-linear spectral contraction with acceleration around generation 20 (Track II drift of -1536.562, slope -42.621, with 'whole modes' lost). No public code available; we reimplement from paper formulas with effective rank as fallback. Architecture transfer risk: validated only on OPT-125M with frozen sentence encoder (m=384); beta_hat calibration may need re-tuning for Pythia",
    "Countering Model Collapse in Iterative Self-Training via Dynamic Center-Edge Sampling (DCES, MDPI Electronics 15(4):869, Feb 2026) — closest adaptive competitor; uses intra-cluster dispersion to rebalance center-edge sampling within synthetic pool on GPT-2/Qwen/OPT-6.7B. Key distinction: DCES adapts sample selection within synthetic data, while we adapt global real/synthetic mixing ratio based on spectral Gram matrix health",
    "Characterizing Model Behavior Under Synthetic Data Training (arXiv 2510.05133, Oct 2025) — demonstrates calibration degradation (ECE) precedes accuracy loss by 1-2 iterations on Pythia 410M-12B; critically shows degradation accelerates non-linearly beyond 20-25% synthetic proportion (inflection from 0.1-0.2 to 0.4-0.8 pp/pp), providing key evidence for generation-dependent collapse pressure. We include ECE as both a baseline monitoring signal and a competing controller",
    "AI models collapse when trained on recursively generated data (Nature 2024, Shumailov et al.) — establishes the model collapse phenomenon; demonstrates accelerating tail collapse across generations with longer error tails in later generations, providing foundational evidence for non-linear, generation-dependent collapse pressure",
    "Knowledge Collapse in LLMs: When Fluency Survives but Facts Fail under Recursive Synthetic Training (arXiv 2509.04796, Sep 2025) — demonstrates three distinct non-linear collapse stages (preservation -> knowledge collapse -> instruction-following collapse) on GEMMA 3 1B with prompt-dependent transition points; further confirms generation-dependent, non-uniform collapse dynamics",
    "Golden Ratio Weighting Prevents Model Collapse (arXiv 2502.18049, He et al., 2025) — proves optimal static mixing ratio converges to 1/phi approx 0.618; we provide the dynamic counterpart via spectral-guided adaptive mixing that exploits generation-dependent collapse pressure",
    "Preventing Model Collapse Under Overparametrization: Optimal Mixing Ratios (arXiv 2509.22341, Garg et al., 2025) — extends golden ratio to ridge regression/interpolation; our work addresses the gap that static optima cannot adapt to varying collapse pressure",
    "From Collapse to Improvement: Statistical Perspectives on the Evolutionary Dynamics of Iterative Training on Contaminated Sources (arXiv 2602.10531, Feb 2026) — proves decaying real-data mixing weights avoid collapse when sum(alpha_t) = infinity with appropriate sample sizes; provides direct theoretical support for dynamic mixing strategies",
    "A Theoretical Perspective: How to Prevent Model Collapse in Self-consuming Training Loops (arXiv 2502.18865, ICLR 2025) — recursive stability theory showing constant real-data proportion ensures convergence; motivates adaptive optimization of that proportion across generations",
    "RankMe: Assessing the Downstream Performance of Pretrained Self-Supervised Representations by Their Rank (Garrido et al., ICML 2023) — provides architecture-agnostic, hyperparameter-free effective rank metric via spectral entropy of singular values; serves as our primary fallback to SIGMA Track I and mitigates architecture transfer risk",
    "Paired Seed Evaluation: Statistical Reliability for Learning-Based Simulators (arXiv 2512.24145, Dec 2025) — paired seed methodology that provably reduces variance when seed-level correlations are positive; we adopt this to maximize statistical power with 3 seeds per condition",
    "Strong Model Collapse (ICLR 2025) — proves collapse persists even with tiny synthetic fractions under scaling",
    "Entropy Collapse: A Universal Failure Mode of Intelligent Systems (arXiv 2512.12381, 2025) — proves collapse is first-order transition without critical slowing down; motivates using spectral contraction rate rather than bifurcation-based EWS",
    "Is Model Collapse Inevitable? Breaking the Curse of Recursion by Accumulating Real and Synthetic Data (Gerstgrasser et al. 2024) — accumulation baseline strategy",
    "Curated Synthetic Data Doesn't Have to Collapse: A Theoretical Study of Generative Retraining with Pluralistic Preferences (arXiv 2605.07724, Falahati et al., 2026) — concurrent collapse prevention via pluralistic curation; complementary mechanism to our mixing-ratio control approach",
    "Escaping Model Collapse via Synthetic Data Verification (arXiv 2510.16657, 2025) — verification-based prevention; requires oracle verifier unlike our approach",
    "Monitoring Neural Training with Topology: A Footprint-Predictable Collapse Index (arXiv 2604.26984, 2026) — concurrent topology-based monitoring; targets representational anisotropy in single-run fine-tuning, not recursive generation collapse",
    "Pythia: A Suite for Analyzing Large Language Models Across Training and Scaling (ICML 2023) — experimental platform providing 410M (HellaSwag ~40.9%, well above chance) and 1B checkpoints",
    "Data Mixing Laws: Optimizing Data Mixtures by Predicting Language Modeling Performance (arXiv 2403.16952, 2024) — context for data mixing optimization; loss-based rather than spectral-based signals"
  ],
  "description": "We present the first empirical study connecting spectral collapse monitoring to adaptive global real/synthetic data mixing in a closed-loop system during recursive synthetic training. While SIGMA (2026) provides powerful spectral diagnostics for model collapse via embedding Gram matrix log-determinants and theoretical optimal static mixing ratios have been established (converging to 1/phi approx 0.618 in simplified settings), no existing system uses spectral health signals to dynamically adjust global data mixing. Crucially, empirical evidence consistently shows that collapse pressure is non-linear and generation-dependent: SIGMA's S2 experiments demonstrate accelerating spectral contraction with 'whole modes' lost around generation 20; Shumailov et al. (Nature 2024) show compounding tail collapse across generations; arXiv 2510.05133 identifies inflection points at 20-25% synthetic proportion where degradation rates double; and the Knowledge Collapse study (arXiv 2509.04796) confirms three distinct non-linear collapse stages. This non-linearity is precisely what makes static mixing suboptimal and motivates closed-loop control. DCES (2026) provides the closest adaptive approach but operates at the sample selection level within the synthetic pool using semantic cluster dispersion, not at the global mixing ratio level. We reimplement SIGMA's Track I log-determinant bounds as the feedback signal (with effective rank via RankMe as architecture-agnostic fallback, since SIGMA was validated only on OPT-125M and no public code exists) — when the generation-over-generation spectral decline exceeds a calibrated threshold, a proportional controller increases the real data fraction. We include calibration-based monitoring (ECE, following arXiv 2510.05133) as both a baseline signal and a competing controller; if calibration performs comparably to spectral guidance, the paper's primary contribution — the first closed-loop adaptive mixing framework for collapse prevention — remains intact, with signal comparison as a secondary empirical question. The core claim is: given a fixed real data budget, spectral-guided dynamic allocation across generations achieves better collapse prevention than uniform allocation, because early stable generations consume less real data while later collapse-prone generations consume more, concentrating intervention where the empirically established non-linear collapse pressure is highest. Using Pythia 410M (primary, 4 conditions x 5 generations x 3 paired seeds) and Pythia 1B (transfer validation including adaptive controller run), we evaluate spectral-guided mixing, calibration-guided mixing, fixed 50/50, and no-intervention baselines on held-out perplexity, distinct-4, and HellaSwag accuracy (Pythia 410M baseline ~40.9%, well above chance, providing meaningful collapse detection range; MMLU excluded at ~27%, near-random for 410M). We adopt paired seeds across conditions following arXiv 2512.24145 to maximize statistical power, explicitly acknowledging 3 seeds as a limitation for detecting small effect sizes. Estimated at ~100-135 H100-hours, targeting EMNLP 2026 ARR (May 25, 2026).",
  "project_name": "spectral_collapse_ews",
  "source": "web_research"
}
